/* book Worked example: Q: Design the flight of a dog-legged staircase: floor-to-floor height 3.2 m, riser 160 mm, tread 300 mm, landings 1.25 m wide at both ends spanning across the stair hall onto walls. Live load 3 kN/m², finish 1 kN/m², M20, Fe415, 20 mm cover, 12 mm bars.
   1. Steps: 3200/160 = 20 risers, 10 per flight; 9 treads, so the going is 9 × 0.300 = 2.7 m. 2R + T = 620 mm, comfortable.
   2. Effective span (landings span parallel to the risers): 2.7 + 2 × smaller of (1.25/2, 1 m) = 2.7 + 2 × 0.625 = 3.95 m.
   3. Waist: trial t = 180 mm, d = 180 − 20 − 6 = 154 mm. Slope factor = √(160² + 300²)/300 = 1.1333.
   4. Loads per m² of plan: waist 25 × 0.180 × 1.1333 = 5.100; steps 25 × 0.160/2 = 2.00; finish 1.00; live 3.00; w = 11.100 kN/m², wu = 16.650 kN/m². (Taking the flight load over the landing ends, which weigh only 8.5 kN/m², is on the safe side.)
   5. Mu = 16.650 × 3.95²/8 = 32.47 kN·m/m; Vu = 16.650 × 3.95/2 = 32.88 kN/m. Mu,lim = 0.138 × 20 × 1000 × 154² = 65.5 kN·m, so under-reinforced.
   6. Steel: 4.6 Mu/(fck b d²) = 0.3149; Ast = 639 mm²/m. 12 mm bars (113.1 mm²): 1000 × 113.1/639 = 177 mm; provide 12 mm @ 170 mm c/c (665 mm²/m).
   7. Distribution: 0.12 % × 1000 × 180 = 216 mm²/m; 8 mm @ 230 mm c/c across the flight.
   8. Shear: τv = 32.88 × 10³/(1000 × 154) = 0.214 N/mm²; pt = 0.432 %, kτc = 1.24 × 0.447 = 0.555 N/mm². Safe.
   9. Deflection: l/d = 3950/154 = 25.65; fs = 231 N/mm², factor ≈ 1.35, allowed 20 × 1.35 = 26.91. Safe. (A 160 mm waist fails: 29.48 against 24.79.)
   10. Landings: each 1.25 m landing spans across the hall onto the walls as a one-way slab, carrying its own load plus the flight reactions of 32.88 kN/m from each flight it receives.
   Answer: Waist 180 mm; main bars 12 mm @ 170 mm c/c along the slope (665 mm²/m) for Mu = 32.47 kN·m/m; distribution 8 mm @ 230 mm c/c. */
